  "knowledgePattern": "Count intersections on each modulus branch with graph-shape checks",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Remove the modulus in its own regions",
      "body": "<p>Find where the quadratic inside the modulus changes sign. Sketch its separate branches alongside the exponential.</p>",
      "recap": "A modulus reflects negative outputs upwards and leaves nonnegative outputs unchanged.",
      "pitfall": "A picture must distinguish a crossing from a touching point.",
      "pause": "Mark the breakpoints and compare the two functions separately on every region.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "A modulus reflects negative outputs upwards and leaves nonnegative outputs unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Combine values with the shape",
      "body": "<p>Use exact test values to locate crossings, and monotonicity or bending to rule out extra ones. On a positive branch, comparing logarithms can make the shape easier to judge.</p>",
      "recap": "Exponential outputs are positive, and the logarithm is defined only for positive inputs and preserves their order.",
      "pitfall": "Two increasing graphs can cross more than once; monotonicity of both separately is not enough.",
      "pause": "Justify both the existence and the maximum number of crossings on each region before adding them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-exponential-log-graphs",
          "reminder": "Exponential outputs are positive, and the logarithm is defined only for positive inputs and preserves their order."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The modulus changes formula at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Neither breakpoint is a solution. On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> decreases continuously from arbitrarily large values to 0, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow></math> increases from values near 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Hence these branches cross exactly once.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow></math> with zero. Its graph bends strictly downwards: the downward parabola and the negative of an upward-bending exponential both have decreasing secant slopes. Such a graph cannot have three zeros, because between two zeros its graph lies strictly above the joining chord.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (square the two positive quantities), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore there is one crossing between −1 and −1/2, and another at 0; the shape check excludes further crossings on this middle branch.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, taking logarithms preserves equality and gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Each logarithm has the familiar increasing, downward-bending shape; translating its input preserves that shape. Their sum, minus a straight line, still has strictly decreasing secant slopes, so H has at most two zeros.</p><p><strong>5.</strong> One zero is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>91</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>91</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>753571</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>746496</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>9</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>; hence H is positive there. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>, so H is negative. Continuity gives a second crossing between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and 4, and the shape argument excludes any more.</p><p><strong>6.</strong> The three disjoint regions contribute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> real solutions. Answer E. This uses the qualitative shapes of the standard graphs, without differentiating exponentials or logarithms.</p>"
}
